 ],
 "literal": "Sweet fruit that ripens and falls from the mango tree of the paddy field / the vāḷai fish of the pond seizes, the chief of such a town / in our house, speaking boastful words, but in his own house / when hand and leg are raised, raising them / like a mirror image / does whatever is pleasing to the mother of his son.",
 "translation": "The chief of the town where the vāḷai fish in the pond snaps up the sweet mangoes that ripen and fall from the field's mango tree, speaks boastful words in our house. But in his own house, like a reflection in a mirror that raises its hands and legs whenever they are raised, he does only what is pleasing to the mother of his son.",
 "modern": "வயல்வெளியில் உள்ள மாமரத்திலிருந்து பழுத்து விழும் இனிய பழத்தை குளத்து வாளை மீன் கவ்விக்கொள்ளும் ஊரைச் சேர்ந்த தலைவன், எமது வீட்டில் பெரிய வார்த்தைகளைப் பேசுவான். ஆனால், தன் வீட்டில், கையும் காலும் தூக்கினால் தானும் தூக்கும் கண்ணாடிப் பிம்பத்தைப் போல, தன் மகனின் தாய்க்குப் பிடித்தமானவற்றையே செய்வான்.",
 "explanation": "This poem is spoken by the courtesan (parattai) to her friends, mocking the hero (the chief of the agricultural Marutam region). She points out his hypocrisy: while he boasts of his independence and power when visiting her house, he becomes completely submissive at his own home. She compares him to a mirror image (āṭip pāvai) that mindlessly mimics every movement of the person standing before it, showing how he obeys his wife's every command. The natural description of the vāḷai fish easily catching the sweet mango that falls from the tree serves as an implied metaphor (uḷḷuṟai). It suggests that just as the fish effortlessly enjoys the sweet fruit, the wife easily controls the hero. The key words are 'āṭip pāvai' (mirror image) and 'putalvaṉ tāy' (mother of his son), which emphasize his domestic subservience.",
